<div class=3D"ltx_abstract" id=3D"abstract">
<h6 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6><button class=3D"sr=
-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</=
button>
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"id1.1">We study allowing large language models (LL=
Ms) to process arbitrarily long prompts through the lens of inference-time =
scaling. We propose <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=3D"id1.1.1">R=
ecursive Language Models</span> (<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=
=3D"id1.1.2">RLM</span>s), a general inference paradigm that treats long pr=
ompts as part of an external <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D=
"id1.1.3">environment</span> and allows the LLM to <span class=3D"ltx_text =
ltx_font_italic" id=3D"id1.1.4">programmatically</span> examine, decompose,=
 and <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"id1.1.5">recursively ca=
ll itself over</span> snippets of the prompt.
We find that RLMs can successfully process inputs up to two orders of magni=
tude beyond model context windows and, even for shorter prompts, dramatical=
ly outperform the quality of vanilla frontier LLMs and common long-context =
scaffolds across four diverse long-context tasks while having comparable co=
st.
At a small scale, we post-train the first natively recursive language model=
. Our model, <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=3D"id1.1.6">RLM-Qwen=
3-8B</span>, outperforms the underlying Qwen3-8B model by <math alttext=3D"=
28.3\%" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"id1.1.m1" intent=3D":li=
teral"><semantics><mrow><mn>28.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=
=3D"application/x-tex">28.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> on average an=
d even approaches the quality of vanilla GPT-5 on three long-context tasks.=
 Code is available at <a class=3D"ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter" href=
=3D"https://github.com/alexzhang13/rlm" title=3D"">https://github.com/alexz=
hang13/rlm</a>.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;=
">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
