90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span class=3D"ltx_text" id=3D"S1.F1.4.2" sty=
le=3D"font-size:90%;">A comparison of GPT-5 and a corresponding RLM using G=
PT-5 on three long-context tasks of increasing complexity: <span class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold" id=3D"S1.F1.4.2.1">S-NIAH</span>, <span class=3D"ltx=
_text ltx_font_bold" id=3D"S1.F1.4.2.2">OOLONG</span>, and <span class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold" id=3D"S1.F1.4.2.3">OOLONG-Pairs</span>. For each tas=
k, we scale the input length from <math alttext=3D"2^{13}" class=3D"ltx_Mat=
h" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S1.F1.3.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><m=
sup><mn>2</mn><mn>13</mn></msup><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">=
2^{13}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math alttext=3D"2^{18}" class=3D=
"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S1.F1.4.2.m2" intent=3D":literal"><sema=
ntics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>18</mn></msup><annotation encoding=3D"application=
/x-tex">2^{18}</annotation></semantics></math>. GPT-5 performance degrades =
significantly as a function of both input length and task complexity, while=
 the RLM maintains strong performance.
Inputs beyond the red region do not fit in GPT-5=E2=80=99s context window o=
f 272K tokens, but the RLM handles them effectively. Additional experiments=
 across other models and benchmarks are in =C2=A7<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=
=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#S3" title=3D"3 Scaling Long Context=
 Tasks =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref=
_tag">3</span></a>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report i=
ssue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S1.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S1.p1.1">Frontier reasoning models have limited co=
ntext windows and, even within their limits, tend to exhibit <span class=3D=
"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S1.p1.1.1">context rot</span>&nbsp;<cite c=
lass=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Hong<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_=
etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2=
512.24601v2#bib.bib37" title=3D"Context rot: how context degradation affect=
s llm performance">2025</a>)</cite>, a phenomenon illustrated in Figure&nbs=
p;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#S1.F1" t=
itle=3D"Figure 1 =E2=80=A3 1 Introduction =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Mode=
ls"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> where quality degrade=
s steeply as prompts get longer. Though we expect context lengths to steadi=
ly rise through improvements to training, architecture, and infrastructure,=
 we are interested in <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S1.p1.=
1.2">whether it is possible to scale the context size of general-purpose LL=
Ms by orders of magnitude</span>. This is increasingly urgent as LLMs begin=
 to be widely adopted for long-horizon tasks, in which they must routinely =
process tens if not hundreds of millions of tokens.</p><button class=3D"sr-=
only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</b=
utton>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S1.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S1.p2.1">We study this question through the lens o=
f scaling inference-time compute. We are inspired by the way that <span cla=
ss=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S1.p2.1.1">reasoning models</span> ha=
ve become the fundamental interface to LLMs, resulting not only in empirica=
l gains but also additional theoretical expressive power&nbsp;<cite class=
=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Merrill and Sabharwal, <a class=3D"ltx_r=
ef" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib51" title=3D"The exp=
ressive power of transformers with chain of thought">2024</a>)</cite> compa=
red to vanilla Transformers. Though most inference-time methods for dealing=
 with long context are task-specific&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citem=
acro_citep">(Wu<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a cla=
ss=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib52" title=
=3D"Recursively summarizing books with human feedback">2021</a>; Chang<span=
 class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=
=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib53" title=3D"BooookScore: a =
systematic exploration of book-length summarization in the era of LLMs">202=
4</a>)</cite>, the most popular general approach is <span class=3D"ltx_text=
 ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S1.p2.1.2">context condensation</span> or <span cla=
ss=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S1.p2.1.3">compaction</span> <cite cl=
ass=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Khattab<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bi=
b_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html=
/2512.24601v2#bib.bib16" title=3D"Baleen: robust multi-hop reasoning at sca=
le via condensed retrieval">2021</a>; Smith, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"h=
ttps://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib17" title=3D"OpenHands context co=
ndensensation for more efficient ai agents">2025</a>; OpenAI, <a class=3D"l=
tx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib21" title=3D"Cod=
ex cli: a lightweight coding agent for your terminal">2025a</a>; Wu<span cl=
ass=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"=
https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib5" title=3D"ReSum: unlocking lon=
g-horizon search intelligence via context summarization">2025</a>)</cite>, =
where context from user requests or agent trajectories is repeatedly summar=
ized once it exceeds a length threshold. Unfortunately, compaction is rarel=
y expressive enough for tasks that require dense access throughout the prom=
pt. It presumes that <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S1.p2.1=
.4">some</span> details that appear early in the prompt can safely be forgo=
tten to make room for new content.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" styl=
e=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<figure class=3D"ltx_figure" id=3D"S1.F2"><img alt=3D"Refer to caption" cla=
ss=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height=3D"247" id=3D"S1=
.F2.g1" src=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2/figures/Fig2.png" width=
=3D"333">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure"><span class=3D"ltx_text" id=3D"S1.F2.4.2.1" style=3D"font-size:=
90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span class=3D"ltx_text" id=3D"S1.F2.2.1" sty=
le=3D"font-size:90%;">A Recursive Language Model (RLM) treats prompts as pa=
rt of the environment. It loads the input prompt as a variable inside a REP=
L environment <math alttext=3D"\mathcal{E}" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"i=
nline" id=3D"S1.F2.2.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi class=3D"ltx_=
font_mathcaligraphic">=E2=84=B0</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-t=
ex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and writes code to peek int=
o, decompose, and invoke itself recursively over programmatic snippets of t=
he variable.</span></figcaption>
</figure><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report i=
ssue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S1.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S1.p3.1">We introduce <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_=
font_bold" id=3D"S1.p3.1.1">Recursive Language Models</span> (<span class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=3D"S1.p3.1.2">RLM</span>s), a general-purpos=
e inference paradigm for dramatically scaling the effective input and outpu=
t lengths of LLMs. The key insight is that arbitrarily long user prompts sh=
ould not be fed into the neural network (e.g., Transformer) directly but sh=
ould instead be treated as <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S=
1.p3.1.3">part of the environment that the LLM is tasked to <span class=3D"=
ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=3D"S1.p3.1.3.1">symbolically and recursively</sp=
an> interact with</span>.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"disp=
lay: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S1.p4">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S1.p4.4">As Figure&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=
=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#S1.F2" title=3D"Figure 2 =E2=80=A3 =
1 Introduction =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text=
 ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows, an RLM exposes the same external interfac=
e as an LLM or a reasoning model: it accepts a string prompt of arbitrary s=
tructure and produces a string response. Given a prompt <math alttext=3D"P"=
 class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S1.p4.1.m1" intent=3D":literal=
"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">P</annota=
tion></semantics></math>, the RLM initializes a Read-Eval-Print Loop (REPL)=
 programming environment in which <math alttext=3D"P" class=3D"ltx_Math" di=
splay=3D"inline" id=3D"S1.p4.2.m2" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi=
><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></mat=
h> is set as the value of a variable. It then offers the LLM general contex=
t about the REPL environment (e.g., the length of the string <math alttext=
=3D"P" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S1.p4.3.m3" intent=3D":l=
iteral"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">P</=
annotation></semantics></math>), and permits it to write code that peeks in=
to and decomposes <math alttext=3D"P" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline"=
 id=3D"S1.p4.4.m4" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation enc=
oding=3D"application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, and to itera=
tively observe any side effects from execution. Crucially, RLMs encourage t=
he LLM to understand, transform, and execute the input prompt by <span clas=
s=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S1.p4.4.1">writing symbolic programs t=
hat invoke the LLM itself</span> on as many slices of the input as necessar=
y.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issu=
e for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S1.p5">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S1.p5.1">By treating the prompt itself as an exter=
nal object and enabling symbolic recursion, RLMs tackle limitations of expr=
essive power in recent work on coding agents, retrieval agents, and sub-age=
nt delegation. In particular, prior coding agents and retrieval agents trea=
t some designated external data source (e.g., a filesystem or a corpus of s=
earch documents) as an environment for fetching snippets. However, they <sp=
an class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S1.p5.1.1">can only fill up the=
 underlying LLM=E2=80=99s context window with snippets before breaking down=
</span>. Similarly, prior self-delegation approaches&nbsp;<cite class=3D"lt=
x_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Anthropic, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https:=
//arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib22" title=3D"Claude code: subagents =
=E2=80=94 modular ai workflows with isolated agent contexts">2025</a>; Sent=
ient AI, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#b=
ib.bib47" title=3D"ROMA: the backbone for open-source meta-agents">2025</a>=
; Schroeder<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=
=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib27" title=
=3D"THREAD: thinking deeper with recursive spawning">2025</a>; Sun<span cla=
ss=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"h=
ttps://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib25" title=3D"Scaling long-horizon=
 llm agent via context-folding">2025</a>)</cite> allow LLMs to invoke thems=
elves as sub-agents. However, they <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic"=
 id=3D"S1.p5.1.2">are handicapped by the underlying LLM=E2=80=99s limited o=
utput lengths</span> because they are designed to verbalize sub-calls autor=
egressively rather than producing them programmatically.</p><button class=
=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding ele=
ment</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S1.p6">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S1.p6.1">We evaluate RLMs using a frontier closed =
model (GPT-5;&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet">Singh<span =
class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span> <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D=
"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib1" title=3D"OpenAI gpt-5 system=
 card">2025</a></cite>) and a frontier open model (Qwen3-Coder-480B-A35B;&n=
bsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet">Qwen Team <a class=3D"ltx_=
ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib41" title=3D"Qwen3-=
coder-480b-a35b-instruct">2025b</a></cite>) across four tasks with varying =
levels of complexity: deep research&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citema=
cro_citep">(Chen<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a cl=
ass=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib12" titl=
e=3D"BrowseComp-plus: a more fair and transparent evaluation benchmark of d=
eep-research agent">2025</a>)</cite>, information aggregation&nbsp;<cite cl=
ass=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Bertsch<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bi=
b_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html=
/2512.24601v2#bib.bib11" title=3D"Oolong: evaluating long context reasoning=
 and aggregation capabilities">2025</a>)</cite>, code repository understand=
ing&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Bai<span class=3D"lt=
x_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://a=
rxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib8" title=3D"LongBench v2: towards deeper =
understanding and reasoning on realistic long-context multitasks">2025</a>)=
</cite>, and a synthetic pairwise reasoning task where even frontier models=
 fail catastrophically. We compare RLMs against direct LLM calls as well as=
 context compaction, retrieval tool-use agents, and code-generation agents.=
</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue =
for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S1.p7">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S1.p7.1">We find that RLMs demonstrate extremely s=
trong performance even at the 10M+ token scale, and substantially outperfor=
m all other approaches at long-context processing, in many cases by double-=
digit percentage gains while maintaining comparable cost. In particular, as=
 demonstrated in Figure&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org=
/html/2512.24601v2#S1.F1" title=3D"Figure 1 =E2=80=A3 1 Introduction =E2=80=
=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span=
></a>, RLMs exhibit far less severe degradation for longer contexts and mor=
e sophisticated tasks.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display=
: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S1.p8">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S1.p8.1">Finally, at a small scale, we post-train =
the first natively recursive language model, demonstrating that RLMs can be=
 improved quickly with little additional training. While a small open model=
 (Qwen3-8B;&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet">Yang<span cla=
ss=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span> <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"ht=
tps://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib44" title=3D"Qwen3 technical repor=
t">2025</a></cite>) struggles to solve long context tasks even in an RLM sc=
affold, our simple general-purpose training recipe uses only 1,000 samples =
from unrelated domains to improve its performance by a median of <math altt=
ext=3D"28.3\%" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S1.p8.1.m1" inte=
nt=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>28.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation =
encoding=3D"application/x-tex">28.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> acros=
s the four evaluation tasks.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"d=
isplay: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_section" id=3D"S2">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Recursive Language Models<=
/h2><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue =
for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S2.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S2.p1.10">Given a base neural language model <math=
 alttext=3D"\mathcal{M}" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p1.=
1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi class=3D"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic"=
>=E2=84=B3</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</anno=
tation></semantics></math> with maximum context size <math alttext=3D"K" cl=
ass=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p1.2.m2" intent=3D":literal"><=
semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">K</annotatio=
n></semantics></math>, a Recursive Language Model (RLM) is an inference-tim=
e scaffold around <math alttext=3D"\mathcal{M}" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=
=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p1.3.m3" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi class=3D"l=
tx_font_mathcaligraphic">=E2=84=B3</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/=
x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> that treats the user pro=
mpt as part of the environment without giving up the ability to densely pro=
cess its content through different calls to <math alttext=3D"\mathcal{M}" c=
lass=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p1.4.m4" intent=3D":literal">=
<semantics><mi class=3D"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">=E2=84=B3</mi><annotation=
 encoding=3D"application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>=
.
Given an arbitrary-length prompt string <math alttext=3D"P\in\Sigma^{\star}=
" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p1.5.m5" intent=3D":litera=
l"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=E2=88=88</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=3D"no=
rmal">=CE=A3</mi><mo>=E2=8B=86</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"ap=
plication/x-tex">P\in\Sigma^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, an RLM=
 interacts with a persistent external environment <math alttext=3D"\mathcal=
{E}" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p1.6.m6" intent=3D":lit=
eral"><semantics><mi class=3D"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">=E2=84=B0</mi><anno=
tation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics><=
/math> and returns a response string <math alttext=3D"Y\in\Sigma^{\star}" c=
lass=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p1.7.m7" intent=3D":literal">=
<semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=E2=88=88</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=3D"norma=
l">=CE=A3</mi><mo>=E2=8B=86</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"appli=
cation/x-tex">Y\in\Sigma^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> (Figure&nb=
sp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#S1.F2" =
title=3D"Figure 2 =E2=80=A3 1 Introduction =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Mod=
els"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
We would like effectively <em class=3D"ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S2.p=
1.10.1">unbounded input tokens</em> (<math alttext=3D"|P|\gg K" class=3D"lt=
x_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p1.8.m8" intent=3D":literal"><semantics=
><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">=
|</mo></mrow><mo>=E2=89=AB</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"app=
lication/x-tex">|P|\gg K</annotation></semantics></math>), <em class=3D"ltx=
_emph ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S2.p1.10.2">unbounded output tokens</em>, and =
an <em class=3D"ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S2.p1.10.3">unbounded seman=
tic horizon</em>, e.g. the ability to do <math alttext=3D"\Omega(|P|)" clas=
s=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p1.9.m9" intent=3D":literal"><se=
mantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=3D"normal">=CE=A9</mi><mo lspace=3D"0em" rspa=
ce=3D"0em">=E2=80=8B</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><mrow><mo stret=
chy=3D"false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">|</mo></mrow><mo stret=
