<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S2.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S2.p2.2">Algorithm&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=
=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#alg1" title=3D"Algorithm 1 =E2=80=
=A3 2 Recursive Language Models =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span =
class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> describes how an RLM achieves t=
his. Given a prompt <math alttext=3D"P" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inlin=
e" id=3D"S2.p2.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation e=
ncoding=3D"application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, the RLM in=
itializes a persistent REPL programming environment with a variable contain=
ing the user prompt as a string and a function for invoking a sub-RLM with =
a new prompt. Then, it starts the RLM loop. In the first iteration, the alg=
orithm invokes the <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S2.p2.2.1=
">root</span> neural model <math alttext=3D"\mathcal{M}" class=3D"ltx_Math"=
 display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p2.2.m2" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi cl=
ass=3D"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">=E2=84=B3</mi><annotation encoding=3D"appl=
ication/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> with only (const=
ant-size) metadata about the user prompt, like its length, a short prefix, =
and how to access parts of it.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D=
"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S2.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S2.p3.1">The root is instructed via prompting (App=
endix&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2=
#A3" title=3D"Appendix C Additional Methods and Baseline Details =E2=80=A3 =
Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a=
>) and/or fine-tuning (Appendix&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://a=
rxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#A1" title=3D"Appendix A Additional Training Deta=
ils =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_ta=
g">A</span></a>) to operate like an RLM: that is, to <span class=3D"ltx_tex=
t ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S2.p3.1.1">generate code that helps it understand =
and transform its parts of its prompt <math alttext=3D"P" class=3D"ltx_Math=
" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p3.1.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi=
>P</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics=
></math></span>, and to build up intermediate values and the final response=
 into new variables, potentially by <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic=
" id=3D"S2.p3.1.2">invoking the sub-RLM within loops</span>. In Section&nbs=
p;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#S4" titl=
e=3D"4 Results and Discussion =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span cl=
ass=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we find that existing LLMs can b=
e prompted to do this and that training an 8B model to be natively recursiv=
e is promising.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;=
">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S2.p4">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S2.p4.1">Each iteration of the RLM loop executes c=
ode in the REPL, updates REPL state (intermediate variables), and collects =
in <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" id=3D"S2.p4.1.1">stdout</sp=
an> any printed text. Only (constant-size) metadata about <span class=3D"lt=
x_text ltx_font_typewriter" id=3D"S2.p4.1.2">stdout</span>, like a short pr=
efix and length, is appended to <math alttext=3D"\mathcal{M}" class=3D"ltx_=
Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p4.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><=
mi class=3D"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">=E2=84=B3</mi><annotation encoding=3D=
"application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>=E2=80=99s h=
istory for the next iteration.<span class=3D"ltx_note ltx_role_footnote" id=
=3D"footnote1"><sup class=3D"ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class=3D"ltx_note_=
outer"><span class=3D"ltx_note_content"><sup class=3D"ltx_note_mark">1</sup=
><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>This is key: it forces <math =
alttext=3D"\mathcal{M}" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"footnot=
e1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi class=3D"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic=
">=E2=84=B3</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</ann=
otation></semantics></math> to rely on variables and sub-calls to manage lo=
ng strings instead of polluting its window. In principle, if we trim each t=
urn to <math alttext=3D"c" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"foot=
note1.m2" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding=3D"=
application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, we will have a=
t most <math alttext=3D"K/c" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"fo=
otnote1.m3" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>c<=
/mi></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">K/c</annotation></sem=
antics></math> root iterations, each of which can launch arbitrarily many s=
ub-calls. This is not a fundamental limitation, e.g. one could move the roo=
t horizon itself into a variable, but we typically want to limit the iterat=
ions at any level of recursion irrespective.</span></span></span> Once the =
RLM sets the variable <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" id=3D"S2=
.p4.1.3">Final</span> inside the REPL, iteration stops and the value in <sp=
an class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" id=3D"S2.p4.1.4">Final</span> is =
returned as the response.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"disp=
lay: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S2.p5">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S2.p5.1">RLMs make three simple design choices tha=
t are missing from existing scaffolds. To highlight these, we include Algor=
ithm&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#=
alg2" title=3D"Algorithm 2 =E2=80=A3 2 Recursive Language Models =E2=80=A3 =
Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a=
> to illustrate a deceptively =E2=80=9Csimilar=E2=80=9D algorithm that is f=
ar less expressive. Both algorithms support some notion of sub-calls, exter=
nal objects, and code execution, but they differ in terms of where the prom=
pt and intermediate values live and where recursion occurs.</p><button clas=
s=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding el=
ement</button>
</div>
