<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S2.p6">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S2.p6.4">First, an RLM must give the underlying LL=
M <math alttext=3D"\mathcal{M}" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D=
"S2.p6.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi class=3D"ltx_font_mathcalig=
raphic">=E2=84=B3</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">\mathcal{M=
}</annotation></semantics></math> a <em class=3D"ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" =
id=3D"S2.p6.4.1">symbolic handle</em> to the user prompt <math alttext=3D"P=
" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p6.2.m2" intent=3D":litera=
l"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">P</annot=
ation></semantics></math>, so the model can manipulate it without copying t=
ext into the root context window. Instead, ineffective Algorithm&nbsp;<a cl=
ass=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#alg2" title=3D"=
Algorithm 2 =E2=80=A3 2 Recursive Language Models =E2=80=A3 Recursive Langu=
age Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> starts by put=
ting the user prompt <math alttext=3D"P" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inli=
ne" id=3D"S2.p6.3.m3" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation =
encoding=3D"application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> into the L=
LM context window (<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" id=3D"S2.p6=
.4.2">hist</span>) and thus inherits the window limitations of <math alttex=
t=3D"\mathcal{M}" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p6.4.m4" i=
ntent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi class=3D"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">=E2=84=
=B3</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation>=
</semantics></math> and falls back to heuristics like context compaction. E=
ven though the scaffold can access external data with, say, a <span class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" id=3D"S2.p6.4.3">Search</span> action or =
filesystem access, it is fatally bounded with respect to user input.</p><bu=
tton class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for pre=
ceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S2.p7">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S2.p7.2">Second, ineffective Algorithm&nbsp;<a cla=
ss=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#alg2" title=3D"A=
lgorithm 2 =E2=80=A3 2 Recursive Language Models =E2=80=A3 Recursive Langua=
ge Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> asks <math alt=
text=3D"\mathcal{M}" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p7.1.m1=
" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi class=3D"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">=E2=
=84=B3</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotati=
on></semantics></math> to autoregressively generate the output directly, vi=
a a <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" id=3D"S2.p7.2.1">Finish</s=
pan> action. This may seem innocuous, but it means that it also cannot gene=
rate longer outputs than the context window of <math alttext=3D"\mathcal{M}=
" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p7.2.m2" intent=3D":litera=
l"><semantics><mi class=3D"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">=E2=84=B3</mi><annotat=
ion encoding=3D"application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></ma=
th> permits.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">R=
eport issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S2.p8">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S2.p8.6">Third, and perhaps most importantly, an R=
LM requires <em class=3D"ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S2.p8.6.1">symboli=
c recursion</em>. That is, code running <em class=3D"ltx_emph ltx_font_ital=
ic" id=3D"S2.p8.6.2">inside</em> <math alttext=3D"\mathcal{E}" class=3D"ltx=
_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p8.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics>=
<mi class=3D"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">=E2=84=B0</mi><annotation encoding=
=3D"application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> must be =
able to invoke <math alttext=3D"\mathcal{M}" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"=
inline" id=3D"S2.p8.2.m2" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi class=3D"ltx_f=
ont_mathcaligraphic">=E2=84=B3</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-te=
x">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> on programmatically construc=
ted transformations of <math alttext=3D"P" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"in=
line" id=3D"S2.p8.3.m3" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotatio=
n encoding=3D"application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., i=
nside arbitrarily large loops), storing intermediate results symbolically. =
Though Algorithm&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2=
512.24601v2#alg2" title=3D"Algorithm 2 =E2=80=A3 2 Recursive Language Model=
s =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag"=
>2</span></a> includes both a code execution action and a =E2=80=9Csub-LLM=
=E2=80=9D action separately, it is not able to invoke the sub-LLM programma=
tically and hence can only delegate a few <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_=
italic" id=3D"S2.p8.6.3">explicitly verbalized tasks</span> rather than wri=
ting short programs that can, say, loop over slices of the prompt and launc=
h <math alttext=3D"\Omega(|P|)" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D=
"S2.p8.4.m4" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=3D"normal=
">=CE=A9</mi><mo lspace=3D"0em" rspace=3D"0em">=E2=80=8B</mo><mrow><mo stre=
tchy=3D"false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stret=
chy=3D"false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><anno=
tation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">\Omega(|P|)</annotation></semantics><=
/math> or even <math alttext=3D"\Omega(|P|^{2})" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=
=3D"inline" id=3D"S2.p8.5.m5" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi math=
variant=3D"normal">=CE=A9</mi><mo lspace=3D"0em" rspace=3D"0em">=E2=80=8B</=
mo><mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false">|<=
/mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stre=
tchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-te=
x">\Omega(|P|^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> processes to understand =
or transform all parts of <math alttext=3D"P" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D=
"inline" id=3D"S2.p8.6.m6" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annota=
tion encoding=3D"application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p><=
button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for p=
receding element</button>
</div>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_section" id=3D"S3">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Scaling Long Context Tasks=
</h2><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue=
 for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S3.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S3.p1.1">We hypothesize that the effective context=
 window&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Hsieh<span class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"htt=
ps://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib13" title=3D"RULER: what=E2=80=99s =
the real context size of your long-context language models?">2024</a>; Gold=
man<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_re=
f" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib23" title=3D"Is it re=
ally long context if all you need is retrieval? towards genuinely difficult=
 long context nlp">2025</a>; Hong<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et =
al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v=
2#bib.bib37" title=3D"Context rot: how context degradation affects llm perf=
ormance">2025</a>)</cite> of an LLM cannot be understood independently of t=
he <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S3.p1.1.1">specific task<=
/span>. That is, more =E2=80=9Ccomplex=E2=80=9D problems will exhibit degra=
dation at even <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S3.p1.1.2">sh=
orter</span> lengths than simpler ones. Because of this, we must characteri=
ze tasks in terms of how their complexity <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_=
italic" id=3D"S3.p1.1.3">scales with prompt length</span>.</p><button class=
=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding ele=
ment</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S3.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S3.p2.1">For example, needle-in-a-haystack (NIAH) =
problems generally keep =E2=80=98needles=E2=80=99 constant as prompt length=
 is scaled. As a result, frontier models can now reliably solve these tasks=
 in RULER&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Hsieh<span cla=
ss=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"h=
ttps://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib13" title=3D"RULER: what=E2=80=99=
s the real context size of your long-context language models?">2024</a>)</c=
ite> in the 1M+ token settings but struggle at far shorter lengths on OOLON=
G&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Bertsch<span class=3D"=
ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https:/=
/arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib11" title=3D"Oolong: evaluating long co=
ntext reasoning and aggregation capabilities">2025</a>)</cite>, a task wher=
e the answer depends explicitly on almost every line in the prompt.<span cl=
ass=3D"ltx_note ltx_role_footnote" id=3D"footnote2"><sup class=3D"ltx_note_=
mark">2</sup><span class=3D"ltx_note_outer"><span class=3D"ltx_note_content=
"><sup class=3D"ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">=
2</span>This helps explain the patterns seen in Figure&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx=
_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#S1.F1" title=3D"Figure 1 =
=E2=80=A3 1 Introduction =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> earlier: <span class=3D"ltx_text" id=
=3D"footnote2.1">GPT-5</span> scales effectively on the S-NIAH task, where =
the needle size is constant despite longer prompts, but shows faster degrad=
ation at increasingly <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"footno=
te2.2">shorter</span> context lengths on the <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_fo=
nt_italic" id=3D"footnote2.3">linear</span>-complexity OOLONG and the <span=
 class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"footnote2.4">quadratic</span>-com=
plexity OOLONG-Pairs.</span></span></span></p><button class=3D"sr-only butt=
on" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<section class=3D"ltx_subsection" id=3D"S3.SS1">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Tasks</h3><button cla=
ss=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding e=
lement</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S3.SS1.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S3.SS1.p1.1">We design our evaluation around tasks=
 where we can vary the lengths of the prompts, so we can consider problems =
whose difficulties scale differently with context length.</p><button class=
=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding ele=
ment</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S3.SS1.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S3.SS1.p2.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold=
" id=3D"S3.SS1.p2.1.1">S-NIAH</span>. Following the single needle-in-the-ha=
ystack task in RULER&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Hsi=
eh<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref=
" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib13" title=3D"RULER: wh=
at=E2=80=99s the real context size of your long-context language models?">2=
024</a>)</cite>, we consider a set of 50 single tasks that require finding =
a specific phrase or number in a large set of unrelated text. Here, the inf=
ormation being sought scales as <math alttext=3D"O(1)" class=3D"ltx_Math" d=
isplay=3D"inline" id=3D"S3.SS1.p2.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mro=
w><mi>O</mi><mo lspace=3D"0em" rspace=3D"0em">=E2=80=8B</mo><mrow><mo stret=
chy=3D"false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><a=
nnotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></mat=
h> with respect to input length.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=
=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S3.SS1.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S3.SS1.p3.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold=
" id=3D"S3.SS1.p3.1.1">BrowseComp-Plus (1K documents)</span>&nbsp;<cite cla=
ss=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Chen<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_et=
al"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/251=
2.24601v2#bib.bib12" title=3D"BrowseComp-plus: a more fair and transparent =
evaluation benchmark of deep-research agent">2025</a>)</cite>. A multi-hop =
question-answering benchmark for DeepResearch&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite =
ltx_citemacro_citep">(OpenAI, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.or=
g/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib15" title=3D"Deep research">2025b</a>)</cite> qu=
estions that requires reasoning over multiple different documents. The benc=
hmark provides a verified offline corpus that is guaranteed to contain gold=
, evidence, and hard negative documents for each question. Following&nbsp;<=
cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet">Sun<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_=
bib_etal"> et al.</span> (<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/ht=
ml/2512.24601v2#bib.bib25" title=3D"Scaling long-horizon llm agent via cont=
ext-folding">2025</a>)</cite>, we use 150 randomly sampled instances as our=
 evaluation set; we provide <math alttext=3D"1000" class=3D"ltx_Math" displ=
ay=3D"inline" id=3D"S3.SS1.p3.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mn>1000=
</mn><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantic=
s></math> randomly chosen documents as input, in which the gold and evidenc=
e documents are guaranteed to exist. We report the percentage of correct an=
swers. The answer to each task requires piecing together information from s=
everal documents, making this harder than <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_=
bold" id=3D"S3.SS1.p3.1.2">S-NIAH</span> despite also requiring a constant =
number of documents.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: =
none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S3.SS1.p4">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S3.SS1.p4.2"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold=
" id=3D"S3.SS1.p4.2.1">OOLONG</span>&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citem=
acro_citep">(Bertsch<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <=
a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib11" =
title=3D"Oolong: evaluating long context reasoning and aggregation capabili=
ties">2025</a>)</cite>. A long reasoning benchmark that requires transformi=
ng chunks of the input semantically, then aggregating these chunks to form =
a final answer. We report scoring based on the original paper, which scores=
 numerical answers as <math alttext=3D"\texttt{score}(\hat{y})=3D0.75^{|y-\=
hat{y}|}" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S3.SS1.p4.1.m1" inten=
t=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class=3D"ltx_mathvariant_monos=
pace">score</mtext><mo lspace=3D"0em" rspace=3D"0em">=E2=80=8B</mo><mrow><m=
o stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><mover accent=3D"true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mov=
er><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=3D</mo><msup><mn>0.75</m=
n><mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false">|</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=E2=88=92</mo><mov=
er accent=3D"true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false=
">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">\te=
xttt{score}(\hat{y})=3D0.75^{|y-\hat{y}|}</annotation></semantics></math> a=
nd other answers as exact match. We focus specifically on the <span class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" id=3D"S3.SS1.p4.2.2">trec_coarse</span> s=
plit, a set of <math alttext=3D"50" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" i=
d=3D"S3.SS1.p4.2.m2" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation =
encoding=3D"application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> tasks ove=
r a dataset of questions with semantic labels. Each task requires using nea=
rly all entries of the dataset, and therefore scales linearly in processing=
 complexity relative to the input length.</p><button class=3D"sr-only butto=
n" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S3.SS1.p5">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S3.SS1.p5.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold=
" id=3D"S3.SS1.p5.1.1">OOLONG-Pairs</span>. We modify the <span class=3D"lt=
x_text ltx_font_typewriter" id=3D"S3.SS1.p5.1.2">trec_coarse</span> split o=
f OOLONG to include <math alttext=3D"20" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inli=
ne" id=3D"S3.SS1.p5.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annota=
tion encoding=3D"application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> new =
queries that specifically require aggregating <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_f=
ont_italic" id=3D"S3.SS1.p5.1.3">pairs</span> of chunks to construct the fi=
nal answer. We report F1 scores over the answer. Each task requires using n=
early all <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S3.SS1.p5.1.4">pai=
rs</span> of entries of the dataset, and therefore requires processing quad=
ratically-many items relative to the input length. In Appendix&nbsp;<a clas=
s=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#A4.SS1" title=3D"=
D.1 OOLONG-Pairs Benchmark =E2=80=A3 Appendix D Additional Benchmark Detail=
s =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag"=
>D.1</span></a>, we provide all queries in this benchmark.</p><button class=
=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding ele=
ment</button>
</div>
