<section class=3D"ltx_subsection" id=3D"S3.SS2">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Methods and Baselines=
</h3><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue=
 for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S3.SS2.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S3.SS2.p1.1">We compare RLMs against commonly used=
 task-agnostic inference methods, using two modern LMs, GPT-5 with medium r=
easoning&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Singh<span clas=
s=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"ht=
tps://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib1" title=3D"OpenAI gpt-5 system ca=
rd">2025</a>)</cite> and default sampling parameters, and Qwen3-Coder-480B-=
A35B&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Yang<span class=3D"=
ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https:/=
/arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib44" title=3D"Qwen3 technical report">20=
25</a>)</cite> using the sampling parameters described in&nbsp;<cite class=
=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet">Qwen Team (<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"h=
ttps://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib41" title=3D"Qwen3-coder-480b-a35=
b-instruct">2025b</a>)</cite>. For Qwen3-Coder-480B-A35B, we compute costs =
based on the compute provider Fireworks&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_ci=
temacro_citep">(Fireworks AI, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.or=
g/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib42" title=3D"Qwen3 coder 480b a35b instruct">202=
5</a>)</cite>. In addition to evaluating the base model on all tasks, we al=
so evaluate the following methods and baselines:</p><button class=3D"sr-onl=
y button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</butt=
on>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S3.SS2.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S3.SS2.p2.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold=
" id=3D"S3.SS2.p2.1.1">CodeAct (+ BM25).</span> We compare directly to a Co=
deAct&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Wang<span class=3D=
"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https:=
//arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib10" title=3D"Executable code actions e=
licit better llm agents">2024</a>)</cite> agent that can execute code insid=
e of a ReAct&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Yao<span cl=
ass=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"=
https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib7" title=3D"ReAct: synergizing r=
easoning and acting in language models">2023</a>)</cite> loop. Unlike an RL=
M, CodeAct does not offload the user prompt to the code environment, and in=
stead provides it directly to the LM. Furthermore, following&nbsp;<cite cla=
ss=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet">Jimenez<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_=
etal"> et al.</span> (<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2=
512.24601v2#bib.bib3" title=3D"SWE-bench: can language models resolve real-=
world github issues?">2024</a>); Chen<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal">=
 et al.</span> (<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24=
601v2#bib.bib12" title=3D"BrowseComp-plus: a more fair and transparent eval=
uation benchmark of deep-research agent">2025</a>)</cite>, we equip this ag=
ent with a BM25&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Robertso=
n and Zaragoza, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24=
601v2#bib.bib24" title=3D"The probabilistic relevance framework: bm25 and b=
eyond">2009</a>)</cite> retriever that indexes the input context for tasks =
where a retriever is appropriate.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=
=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S3.SS2.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S3.SS2.p3.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold=
" id=3D"S3.SS2.p3.1.1">CodeAct with sub-calls.</span> To specifically ablat=
e offloading the context as a variable in the REPL, we evaluate a CodeAct&n=
bsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Wang<span class=3D"ltx_te=
xt ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv=
.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib10" title=3D"Executable code actions elicit b=
etter llm agents">2024</a>)</cite> baseline with the ability to invoke sub-=
LM calls. Compared to RLMs, this method loads the context directly into the=
 model.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report=
 issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S3.SS2.p4">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S3.SS2.p4.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold=
" id=3D"S3.SS2.p4.1.1">Summary agent.</span> Following&nbsp;<cite class=3D"=
ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet">Sun<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et =
al.</span> (<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v=
2#bib.bib25" title=3D"Scaling long-horizon llm agent via context-folding">2=
025</a>); Wu<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span> (<a class=
=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib5" title=3D=
"ReSum: unlocking long-horizon search intelligence via context summarizatio=
n">2025</a>); Yu<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span> (<a cl=
ass=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib6" title=
=3D"MemAgent: reshaping long-context llm with multi-conv rl-based memory ag=
ent">2025</a>)</cite>, we consider an iterative agent that compacts the con=
text as it is filled. For example, given a corpus of documents, it will ite=
ratively accumulate the documents and summarize when full. In cases where a=
 single document exceeds the model window, the agent will chunk it to fit w=
ithin the model context window and invoke the same strategy over these chun=
ks. For the GPT-5 experiments, due to the extremely high cost of applying t=
his strategy to millions of tokens, we use GPT-5-nano for compaction and GP=
T-5 to provide the final answer.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=
=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S3.SS2.p5">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S3.SS2.p5.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold=
" id=3D"S3.SS2.p5.1.1">RLM with REPL</span>. We implement an RLM with a Pyt=
hon REPL environment, which loads a module for querying a sub-LM and uses a=
 system prompt presented in Appendix&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"http=
s://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#A3" title=3D"Appendix C Additional Methods =
and Baseline Details =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"lt=
x_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. For the GPT-5 experiments, we use GPT-5-m=
ini for the recursive LMs and GPT-5 for the root LM, as we found this choic=
e to strike a good balance between the capabilities of RLMs and the cost of=
 the recursive calls. We notate a RLM using a model as RLM(model), e.g. RLM=
(GPT-5).</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Repor=
t issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S3.SS2.p6">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S3.SS2.p6.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold=
" id=3D"S3.SS2.p6.1.1">RLM with REPL, no sub-calls</span>. We provide an ab=
lation of our method, in which the prompt is loaded in a REPL environment w=
ithout the ability to invoke sub-LM calls.</p><button class=3D"sr-only butt=
on" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S3.SS2.p7">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S3.SS2.p7.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold=
" id=3D"S3.SS2.p7.1.1">Finetuning.</span> To create <span class=3D"ltx_text=
 ltx_font_bold" id=3D"S3.SS2.p7.1.2">RLM-Qwen3-8B</span>, we finetune Qwen3=
-8B on 1,000 filtered trajectories of Qwen3-Coder-480B-A35B as an RLM with =
Qwen3-8B sub-calls on LongBenchPro&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemac=
ro_citep">(Chen<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a cla=
ss=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib55" title=
=3D"LongBench pro: a more realistic and comprehensive bilingual long-contex=
t evaluation benchmark">2026</a>)</cite> tasks. We use sampling parameters =
described in&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet">Qwen Team (<=
a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib43" =
title=3D"Qwen3-8b">2025a</a>)</cite>, and evaluate the fine-tuned RLM-Qwen3=
-8B as an RLM on our long context tasks. The key insight for training is th=
at being an effective sub-call model is roughly similar to being a general =
purpose reasoning model, so we can make the training much more tractable (a=
nd seemingly short-horizon) at small scale by focusing on improving the roo=
t model=E2=80=99s ability to manipulate the REPL and to launch recursive ca=
lls. We provide more training details in Appendix&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref"=
 href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#A1" title=3D"Appendix A Additi=
onal Training Details =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" styl=
e=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
</section>
