<annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">23.1\%</annotation></semantics><=
/math> respectively, highlighting the emergent capability of RLMs to handle=
 extremely information-dense tasks.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" sty=
le=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S4.p4">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.p4.3"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=
=3D"S4.p4.3.1">Observation 2: The REPL is necessary for handling long input=
s, while the recursive sub-calling of RLMs provides strong benefits on info=
rmation-dense inputs.</span> A key characteristic of RLMs is offloading the=
 context as a variable in an environment <math alttext=3D"\mathcal{E}" clas=
s=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S4.p4.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><se=
mantics><mi class=3D"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">=E2=84=B0</mi><annotation en=
coding=3D"application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> th=
at the model can interact with. Even without sub-calling capabilities, our =
ablation of the RLM is able to scale beyond the context limit of the model =
and outperform other task-agnostic baselines on most long context settings.=
 On the CodeQA and BrowseComp+ tasks with Qwen3-Coder, this ablation is abl=
e to outperform the RLM by <math alttext=3D"17.9\%" class=3D"ltx_Math" disp=
lay=3D"inline" id=3D"S4.p4.2.m2" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1=
7.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">17.9\%<=
/annotation></semantics></math> and <math alttext=3D"3\%" class=3D"ltx_Math=
" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S4.p4.3.m3" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow=
><mn>3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">3\%<=
/annotation></semantics></math> respectively.</p><button class=3D"sr-only b=
utton" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S4.p5">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.p5.2">On information-dense tasks like OOLONG or=
 OOLONG-Pairs, we observed several cases where recursive LM sub-calling is =
necessary. In =C2=A7<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/251=
2.24601v2#S4.SS1" title=3D"4.1 Emergent Patterns in RLM Trajectories =E2=80=
=A3 4 Results and Discussion =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span cla=
ss=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we see RLM(Qwen3-Coder) perform=
 the necessary semantic transformation line-by-line through recursive sub-c=
alls, while the ablation without sub-calls is forced to use keyword heurist=
ics to solve these tasks. Across all information-dense tasks, RLMs outperfo=
rm the ablation without sub-calling by <math alttext=3D"10\%" class=3D"ltx_=
Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S4.p5.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><=
mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex"=
>10\%</annotation></semantics></math>-<math alttext=3D"59\%" class=3D"ltx_M=
ath" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S4.p5.2.m2" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><m=
row><mn>59</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">=
59\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" s=
tyle=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S4.p6">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.p6.3"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=
=3D"S4.p6.3.1">Observation 3: LM performance degrades as a function of inpu=
t length and problem complexity, while RLM performance scales better.</span=
> The benchmarks S-NIAH, OOLONG, and OOLONG-Pairs contain a fixed number of=
 tasks over contexts with lengths ranging from <math alttext=3D"2^{13}" cla=
ss=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S4.p6.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><s=
emantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>13</mn></msup><annotation encoding=3D"applicat=
ion/x-tex">2^{13}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math alttext=3D"2^{18=
}" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S4.p6.2.m2" intent=3D":liter=
al"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>18</mn></msup><annotation encoding=3D"ap=
plication/x-tex">2^{18}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each benchmark can=
 be loosely categorized by different processing complexity of the input con=
text with respect to length (roughly constant, linear, and quadratic respec=
tively). In Figure&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html=
/2512.24601v2#S1.F1" title=3D"Figure 1 =E2=80=A3 1 Introduction =E2=80=A3 R=
ecursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>=
, we directly compare an RLM using GPT-5 to base GPT-5 on each task. We fin=
d that GPT-5 performance degrades significantly faster for more complex tas=
ks, while RLM performance degrades at a much slower rate, which aligns with=
 the findings of <cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet">Goldman<span =
class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span> (<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=
=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib23" title=3D"Is it really lo=
ng context if all you need is retrieval? towards genuinely difficult long c=
ontext nlp">2025</a>)</cite>. For context lengths beyond <math alttext=3D"2=
^{14}" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S4.p6.3.m3" intent=3D":l=
iteral"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>14</mn></msup><annotation encoding=
=3D"application/x-tex">2^{14}</annotation></semantics></math>, the RLM cons=
istently outperforms GPT-5.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"di=
splay: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S4.p7">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.p7.1">Furthermore, RLM costs scale proportional=
ly to the complexity of the task, while still remaining in the same order o=
f magnitude of cost as GPT-5 (see Figure&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"=
https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#A6.F11" title=3D"Figure 11 =E2=80=A3 Ap=
pendix F Additional Runtime and Cost Analysis of RLMs =E2=80=A3 Recursive L=
anguage Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> in Appen=
dix&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#A=
6" title=3D"Appendix F Additional Runtime and Cost Analysis of RLMs =E2=80=
=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span=
></a>). In =C2=A7<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.2=
4601v2#S4.SS1" title=3D"4.1 Emergent Patterns in RLM Trajectories =E2=80=A3=
 4 Results and Discussion =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we explore the choices that the R=
LM makes that cause these differences in cost. Lastly, in this setting, we =
also observe that the base LM outperforms RLM in the small input context re=
gime. By construction, a RLM has strictly more representation capacity than=
 an LM. In practice, however, we observe that RLM performance is slightly w=
orse on smaller input lengths, suggesting a tradeoff point between when to =
use a base LM and when to use an RLM.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" s=
tyle=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S4.p8">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.p8.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=
=3D"S4.p8.1.1">Observation 4: The inference cost of RLMs remains comparable=
 to a base LM call but has high variance due to differences in trajectory l=
engths.</span> RLMs iteratively interact with their context until they find=
 a suitable answer, leading to large differences in iteration length depend=
ing on task complexity. In Figure&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https:/=
/arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#S4.F3" title=3D"Figure 3 =E2=80=A3 4 Results a=
nd Discussion =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text =
ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we plot the quartile costs for each method acros=
s all experiments in Table&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.=
org/html/2512.24601v2#S3.T1" title=3D"Table 1 =E2=80=A3 3.1 Tasks =E2=80=A3=
 3 Scaling Long Context Tasks =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span cl=
ass=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> excluding BrowseComp-Plus (1K), a=
s the base models cannot fit any of these tasks in context. For GPT-5, the =
median RLM run is cheaper than the median base model run, but many outlier =
RLM runs are significantly more expensive than any base model query. Howeve=
r, compared to the summarization agent which ingests the entire input conte=
xt, RLMs are up to <math alttext=3D"3\times" class=3D"ltx_math_unparsed" di=
splay=3D"inline" id=3D"S4.p8.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn=
>3</mn><mo lspace=3D"0.222em">=C3=97</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"app=
lication/x-tex">3\times</annotation></semantics></math> cheaper while maint=
aining stronger performance across all tasks because the RLM is able to sel=
ectively view context.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display=
: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S4.p9">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.p9.1">We additionally report runtime numbers of=
 each method in Figures&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org=
/html/2512.24601v2#A6.F7" title=3D"Figure 7 =E2=80=A3 Appendix F Additional=
 Runtime and Cost Analysis of RLMs =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><sp=
an class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>,&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" h=
ref=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#A6.F8" title=3D"Figure 8 =E2=80=
=A3 Appendix F Additional Runtime and Cost Analysis of RLMs =E2=80=A3 Recur=
sive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> in =
Appendix&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.2460=
1v2#A6" title=3D"Appendix F Additional Runtime and Cost Analysis of RLMs =
=E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F=
</span></a>, but we note several important caveats. Unlike API costs, these=
 numbers are heavily dependent on implementation details such as the machin=
e used, API request latency, and the asynchrony of LM calls. In our impleme=
ntation of the baselines and RLMs, all LM calls are blocking / sequential. =
Nevertheless, similar to costs, we observe a wide range of runtimes, especi=
ally for RLMs.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;"=
>Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S4.p10">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.p10.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" i=
d=3D"S4.p10.1.1">Observation 5: RLMs are a model-agnostic inference strateg=
y, but different models exhibit different overall decisions on context mana=
gement and sub-calling.</span> While GPT-5 and Qwen3-Coder-480B both exhibi=
t strong performance as RLMs relative to their base model and other baselin=
es, they also exhibit different performance and behavior across all tasks. =
On BrowseComp-Plus (1k) in particular, RLM(GPT-5) nearly solves all tasks w=
hile RLM(Qwen3-Coder) struggles to solve half.</p><button class=3D"sr-only =
button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button=
>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S4.p11">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.p11.1">We note that the RLM system prompt is fi=
xed for each model across all experiments and is not tuned for any particul=
ar benchmark. Between GPT-5 and Qwen3-Coder, the only difference in the pro=
mpt is an extra line in the RLM(Qwen3-Coder) prompt warning against using t=
oo many sub-calls (see Appendix&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://a=
rxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#A3" title=3D"Appendix C Additional Methods and B=
aseline Details =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_tex=
t ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>). We provide an explicit example of this differ=
ence in example&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/25=
12.24601v2#A5.SS3" title=3D"E.3 RLM(Qwen3-Coder) on OOLONG-Query_212 =E2=80=
=A3 Appendix E Additional RLM Trajectories =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Mod=
els"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a>, where RLM(Qwen3-C=
oder) launches a sub-call per line in OOLONG while GPT-5 is conservative ab=
out sub-querying LMs.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display:=
 none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S4.p12">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.p12.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" i=
d=3D"S4.p12.1.1">Observation 6: Training RLMs on one domain can improve gen=
eral downstream RLM performance.</span> Certain behavior in RLM trajectorie=
s are common among different domains, such as probing the input and recursi=
vely sub-calling on shorter contexts. In Table&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" hr=
ef=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#S3.T1" title=3D"Table 1 =E2=80=A3=
 3.1 Tasks =E2=80=A3 3 Scaling Long Context Tasks =E2=80=A3 Recursive Langu=
age Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we find that=
 <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=3D"S4.p12.1.2">RLM-Qwen3-8B</spa=
n>, a Qwen3-8B model that we fine-tuned on RLM(Qwen3-Coder-480B-A35B) traje=
ctories on a small, <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S4.p12.1=
.3">unrelated</span> set of tasks (LongBenchPro;&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_ci=
te ltx_citemacro_citet">Chen<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</=
span> <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.=
bib55" title=3D"LongBench pro: a more realistic and comprehensive bilingual=
 long-context evaluation benchmark">2026</a></cite>) considerably outperfor=
ms the base Qwen3-8B as an RLM by <math alttext=3D"28.3\%" class=3D"ltx_Mat=
h" display=3D"inline" id=3D"S4.p12.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mr=
ow><mn>28.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex"=
>28.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> on average. Furthermore, its infere=
nce costs are much lower due to better decision making and fewer mistakes a=
s an RLM.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Repo=
rt issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<section class=3D"ltx_subsection" id=3D"S4.SS1">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Emergent Patterns in =
RLM Trajectories</h3><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: non=
e;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S4.SS1.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.SS1.p1.1">Even without explicit training, RLMs =
exhibit interesting context and problem decomposition behavior. We select s=
everal examples of snippets from RLM trajectories to understand how they so=
lve long context problems and where they can improve. We discuss particular=
 examples of interesting behavior here, with additional examples in Appendi=
x&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#A5"=
 title=3D"Appendix E Additional RLM Trajectories =E2=80=A3 Recursive Langua=
ge Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p><button cl=
ass=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding =
element</button>
</div>
<figure class=3D"ltx_figure" id=3D"S4.F4"><img alt=3D"Refer to caption" cla=
ss=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height=3D"248" id=3D"S4=
.F4.g1" src=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2/figures/Frame_7.png" wid=
th=3D"443">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure"><span class=3D"ltx_text" id=3D"S4.F4.3.1.1" style=3D"font-size:=
90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span class=3D"ltx_text" id=3D"S4.F4.4.2" sty=
le=3D"font-size:90%;">RLMs have common patterns in their trajectories when =
solving tasks. (a) We frequently observed RLMs filtering and interacting wi=
th their context through <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" id=3D=
"S4.F4.4.2.1">regex</span> code. (b) We found that RLMs can effectively dec=
ompose their context through recursive sub-calls (c) On long-output tasks, =
RLMs are able to solve sub-problems using recursive sub-LM calls and stitch=
 their outputs to form a final output.</span></figcaption>
</figure><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report i=
ssue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S4.SS1.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.SS1.p2.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold=
" id=3D"S4.SS1.p2.1.1">Chunking and recursively sub-calling LMs.</span> RLM=
s defer essentially unbounded-length reasoning chains to sub-LM calls. The =
choice of decomposition can greatly affect task performance, especially for=
 information-dense problems. In our experiments, we did not observe complic=
ated partitioning strategies beyond uniform chunking or keyword searches. I=
n Figure&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.2460=
1v2#S4.F4" title=3D"Figure 4 =E2=80=A3 4.1 Emergent Patterns in RLM Traject=
ories =E2=80=A3 4 Results and Discussion =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Model=
s"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>b, RLM(Qwen3-Coder) chu=
nks by newline in a 1000+ line context from OOLONG.</p><button class=3D"sr-=
only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</b=
utton>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S4.SS1.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.SS1.p3.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold=
" id=3D"S4.SS1.p3.1.1">Filtering input information using code execution bas=
ed on model priors.</span> A key intuition for why the RLM abstraction can =
maintain strong performance on huge inputs without exploding costs is the L=
M=E2=80=99s ability to filter input context without explicitly seeing it. F=
urthermore, model priors enable the RLM to narrow the search space and proc=
ess fewer input tokens. As an example, in Figure&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" =
href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#S4.F4" title=3D"Figure 4 =E2=80=
=A3 4.1 Emergent Patterns in RLM Trajectories =E2=80=A3 4 Results and Discu=
ssion =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_=
tag">4</span></a>a, we observed RLM(GPT-5) using <span class=3D"ltx_text lt=
x_font_typewriter" id=3D"S4.SS1.p3.1.2">regex</span> queries to search for =
chunks containing keywords in the original prompt (e.g. =E2=80=9Cfestival=
=E2=80=9D) and phrases it has a prior about (e.g. =E2=80=9CLa Union=E2=80=
=9D).</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report i=
ssue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S4.SS1.p4">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S4.SS1.p4.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold=
" id=3D"S4.SS1.p4.1.1">Passing recursive LM outputs through variables for l=
ong output tasks.</span> RLMs are able to produce essentially unbounded tok=
ens well beyond the limit of the base LM by returning variables in the REPL=
 as output. Through the REPL, the RLM can iteratively construct these varia=
bles as a mixture of programmatic and sub-(R)LM output calls. We observed t=
his strategy used heavily in OOLONG-Pairs trajectories, where the RLM store=
d the output of sub-LM calls over the input in variables and stitched them =
together to form a final answer (see Figure&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=
=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#S4.F4" title=3D"Figure 4 =E2=80=A3 =
4.1 Emergent Patterns in RLM Trajectories =E2=80=A3 4 Results and Discussio=
n =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag"=
>4</span></a>c).</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none=
;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_section" id=3D"S5">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Related Works</h2><button =
class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for precedin=
g element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S5.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S5.p1.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=
=3D"S5.p1.1.1">Long-Context LM Systems.</span> There have primarily been tw=
o orthogonal directions for long-context management in language model syste=
ms: 1) directly changing the architecture of and retraining the base LM to =
handle longer contexts&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(P=
ress<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_r=
ef" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib29" title=3D"Train s=
hort, test long: attention with linear biases enables input length extrapol=
ation">2022</a>; Gu<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a=
 class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib31" t=
itle=3D"Efficiently modeling long sequences with structured state spaces">2=
022</a>; Munkhdalai<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a=
 class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib30" t=
itle=3D"Leave no context behind: efficient infinite context transformers wi=
th infini-attention">2024</a>)</cite>, and 2) building a scaffold around th=
e LM that implicitly handles the context =E2=80=93 RLMs focus on the latter=
. One popular class of such strategies is <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_=
italic" id=3D"S5.p1.1.2">lossy</span> context management, which uses summar=
ization or truncation to compress the input context at the cost of potentia=
lly losing fine-grained information. For example, MemWalker&nbsp;<cite clas=
s=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Chen<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_eta=
l"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512=
.24601v2#bib.bib28" title=3D"Walking down the memory maze: beyond context l=
imit through interactive reading">2023</a>)</cite> constructs a tree-like d=
ata structure of the input that the LM can navigate when answering long con=
text questions. ReSum&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Wu=
<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" =
href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib5" title=3D"ReSum: unloc=
king long-horizon search intelligence via context summarization">2025</a>)<=
/cite> is another work that adds a summarization tool to periodically compr=
ess the context of a multi-turn agent. Another class of strategies implemen=
t an explicit memory hierarchy in the agent scaffold&nbsp;<cite class=3D"lt=
x_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Packer<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> e=
t al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.2460=
1v2#bib.bib33" title=3D"MemGPT: towards llms as operating systems">2024</a>=
; Chhikara<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D=
"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib35" title=3D"M=
em0: building production-ready ai agents with scalable long-term memory">20=
25</a>; Zhang<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=
=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib34" title=
=3D"G-memory: tracing hierarchical memory for multi-agent systems">2025</a>=
)</cite>. RLMs differ from these works in that all context window managemen=
t is implicitly handled by the LM itself.</p><button class=3D"sr-only butto=
n" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S5.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S5.p2.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=
=3D"S5.p2.1.1">Task Decomposition through sub-LM calls.</span> Many LM-base=
d agents&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Guo<span class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"htt=
ps://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib36" title=3D"Large language model b=
ased multi-agents: a survey of progress and challenges">2024</a>; Anthropic=
, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib2=
2" title=3D"Claude code: subagents =E2=80=94 modular ai workflows with isol=
ated agent contexts">2025</a>)</cite> use multiple, well-placed LM calls to=
 solve a problem; however, many of these calls are placed based on human-en=
gineered workflows. Several methods like ViperGPT&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_c=
ite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Sur=C3=ADs<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> =
et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.246=
01v2#bib.bib19" title=3D"ViperGPT: visual inference via python execution fo=
r reasoning">2023</a>)</cite>, THREAD&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_cite=
macro_citep">(Schroeder<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>=
, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib2=
7" title=3D"THREAD: thinking deeper with recursive spawning">2025</a>)</cit=
e>, DisCIPL&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Grand<span c=
lass=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D=
"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib20" title=3D"Self-steering lang=
uage models">2025</a>)</cite>, ReDel&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citem=
acro_citep">(Zhu<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a cl=
ass=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib18" titl=
e=3D"ReDel: a toolkit for llm-powered recursive multi-agent systems">2024</=
a>)</cite>, Context Folding&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite=
p">(Sun<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"lt=
x_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib25" title=3D"Scal=
ing long-horizon llm agent via context-folding">2025</a>)</cite>, and Agent=
Fold&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Ye<span class=3D"lt=
x_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://a=
rxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib46" title=3D"AgentFold: long-horizon web =
agents with proactive context management">2025</a>)</cite> have explored de=
ferring the choice of sub-LM calls to the LM. These techniques emphasize <s=
pan class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S5.p2.1.2">task</span> decompo=
sition through recursive LM calls, but are unable to handle long context in=
puts beyond the length of the base LM. RLMs, on the other hand, are enabled=
 by an extremely simple intuition (i.e., placing the prompt in the external=
 environment) to <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S5.p2.1.3">=
symbolically</span> manipulate arbitrarily long strings and to iteratively =
refine their recursion via execution feedback from the persistent REPL.</p>=
<button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for =
preceding element</button>
</div>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_section" id=3D"S6">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Limitations and Future Wor=
k</h2><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issu=
e for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S6.p1.1">While RLMs show strong performance on tas=
ks beyond the context window limitations of existing LMs at reasonable infe=
rence costs, evaluations for more difficult and natural long-context proces=
sing tasks and the best mechanisms for implementing RLMs both remain highly=
 under-explored. We focused on synchronous sub-calls inside of a Python REP=
L environment, but we note that alternative strategies involving asynchrono=
us sub-calls and sandboxed REPLs can potentially significantly reduce the r=
untime and inference cost of RLMs. Furthermore, we chose to use a max recur=
sion depth of one (i.e. sub-calls are LMs); while we found strong performan=
ce on existing long-context benchmarks, we believe that future work should =
investigate deeper levels of recursion or even new hybrids between symbolic=
 recursion and neural attention. We include additional limitations and nega=
tive results in Appendix&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.or=
g/html/2512.24601v2#A2" title=3D"Appendix B Negative Results: Things we Tri=
ed that Did Not Work. =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" styl=
e=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S6.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S6.p2.1">Lastly, we focused our experiments on eva=
luating RLMs using <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic" id=3D"S6.p2.1.1=
">existing</span> frontier models, but show initial evidence on a Qwen3-8B =
model that explicitly training a model to be used as a RLM provides very ra=
pid performance improvements, even outside the training domain. We hypothes=
ize that RLM trajectories can be viewed as a form of reasoning&nbsp;<cite c=
lass=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(OpenAI<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bi=
b_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html=
/2512.24601v2#bib.bib50" title=3D"OpenAI o1 system card">2024</a>; DeepSeek=
-AI<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_re=
f" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib54" title=3D"DeepSeek=
-r1: incentivizing reasoning capability in llms via reinforcement learning"=
>2025</a>)</cite>, which can be trained by bootstrapping existing models&nb=
sp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Zelikman<span class=3D"ltx=
_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://ar=
xiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib48" title=3D"STaR: bootstrapping reasoning=
 with reasoning">2022</a>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/h=
tml/2512.24601v2#bib.bib49" title=3D"Quiet-star: language models can teach =
themselves to think before speaking">2024</a>)</cite>. We hope that trainin=
g native RLMs can be treated as a new axis of scale to improve LM performan=
ce on general and long-horizon tasks.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" s=
tyle=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_section" id=3D"S7">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2><button cla=
ss=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding e=
lement</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S7.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S7.p1.1">We introduced Recursive Language Models (=
RLMs), a general inference framework for language models that offloads the =
input context and enables language models to recursively sub-query language=
 models before providing an output. We explored an instantiation of this fr=
amework that offloads the context into a Python REPL environment as a varia=
ble in memory, enabling the LM to reason over its context in code and recur=
sive LM calls, rather than purely in token space. Our results across multip=
le settings and models demonstrated that RLMs are an effective task-agnosti=
c paradigm for both long-context problems and general reasoning. Building o=
n our small fine-tuning experiments, we are excited to see future work that=
 explicitly trains models to reason as RLMs, which could result in another =
axis of scale for the next generation of language model systems.</p><button=
 class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for precedi=
ng element</button>
</div>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_section" id=3D"S8">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Impact Statement</h2><butt=
on class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for prece=
ding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"S8.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"S8.p1.1">This paper explores a strategy for enabli=
ng language models to solve long context problems and scaling future langua=
ge model systems. The goal is to advance research on systems that can help =
us solve complex problems. While there are potential societal consequences =
of this work, we believe they are not specific to this paper and do not nee=
d to be highlighted here.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"disp=
lay: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
</section>
