<section class=3D"ltx_appendix" id=3D"A1">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Additional Train=
ing Details</h2><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">R=
eport issue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A1.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A1.p1.2">We trained <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_fo=
nt_bold" id=3D"A1.p1.2.1">RLM-Qwen3-8B</span> as a very small scale exercis=
e in training the first natively recursive language model. We hypothesized =
that, though acting as an RLM appears to produce sophisticated behavior due=
 to recursion, it can be sufficient to focus on improving the root LM=E2=80=
=99s ability to interact with the programmatic representation of the prompt=
 in the REPL and to discern when sub-calls are useful.
In other words, while a typical RLM trajectory can be extremely long due to=
 all of the sub-calls potentially launched (possibly <math alttext=3D"\Omeg=
a(|P|)" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"A1.p1.1.m1" intent=3D":=
literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=3D"normal">=CE=A9</mi><mo lspace=
=3D"0em" rspace=3D"0em">=E2=80=8B</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><m=
row><mo stretchy=3D"false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">|</mo></m=
row><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"appl=
ication/x-tex">\Omega(|P|)</annotation></semantics></math> for a prompt <ma=
th alttext=3D"P" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"A1.p1.2.m2" in=
tent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/=
x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>), the leaf sub-calls are essential=
ly general-purpose LLM requests and the major hurdle is learning to operate=
 as the root model.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: n=
one;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A1.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A1.p2.1">This simple insight allowed us to explore=
 a similarly simple recipe for training. In particular, we sampled RLM traj=
ectories from a larger language model (Qwen3-Coder-480B-A35B-Instruct;&nbsp=
;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet">Qwen Team <a class=3D"ltx_ref=
" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib41" title=3D"Qwen3-cod=
er-480b-a35b-instruct">2025b</a></cite>) and, after filtering, distilled th=
em to a smaller model (Qwen3-8B;&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro=
_citet">Qwen Team <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.=
24601v2#bib.bib43" title=3D"Qwen3-8b">2025a</a></cite>) from the same model=
 family.
We evaluated RLM(Qwen3-Coder-480B-A35B) on 750 English LongBenchPro&nbsp;<c=
ite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Chen<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx=
_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/h=
tml/2512.24601v2#bib.bib55" title=3D"LongBench pro: a more realistic and co=
mprehensive bilingual long-context evaluation benchmark">2026</a>)</cite> t=
asks, collecting a total of 2250 candidate trajectories.</p><button class=
=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding ele=
ment</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A1.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A1.p3.1">We first remove trajectories that score e=
xactly 0.0 on the benchmark or do not go beyond one turn, bringing it down =
to 1,072 candidate trajectories. We separated each root RLM turn (i.e. iter=
ation) as a separate SFT sample consisting of an input (the full history) a=
nd output (the output the root LM gave at that step).</p><button class=3D"s=
r-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element<=
/button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A1.p4">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A1.p4.1">We then applied a filtering step to remov=
e turns beyond the context limit of Qwen3-8B (we approximated this as 100k =
characters), and also applied an extra programmatic correction step to fix =
small template mistakes in RLMusage (e.g. outputting final answers, calling=
 the REPL, etc.).
To elaborate, we noticed that trajectories generated by Qwen3-Coder-480B-A3=
5B had noticeable mistakes in following the RLM instructions, which hurt th=
e performance of the distilled RLM-Qwen3-8B. For example, it would often mi=
x FINAL(answer) with FINAL(variable in REPL). We added an extra programmati=
c fixing step to look for common templated mistakes and patch them, leading=
 to much better performance in the final <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_b=
old" id=3D"A1.p4.1.1">RLM-Qwen3-8B</span>. In total, 16% of turns cleaned i=
ncorrectly used FINAL answers, and 13% of turns incorrectly called a variab=
le from the REPL (i.e. FINAL_VAR) as a final answer. In Figure&nbsp;<a clas=
s=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#A1.F5" title=3D"F=
igure 5 =E2=80=A3 Appendix A Additional Training Details =E2=80=A3 Recursiv=
e Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we sh=
ow pre- and post-filtering statistics for our training trajectories.</p><bu=
tton class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for pre=
ceding element</button>
</div>
<figure class=3D"ltx_figure" id=3D"A1.F5"><img alt=3D"Refer to caption" cla=
ss=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height=3D"202" id=3D"A1=
.F5.g1" src=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2/figures/dataset_stats.pn=
g" width=3D"476">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure"><span class=3D"ltx_text" id=3D"A1.F5.3.1.1" style=3D"font-size:=
90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span class=3D"ltx_text" id=3D"A1.F5.4.2" sty=
le=3D"font-size:90%;">We plot statistics for the RLM trajectories on LongBe=
nchPro that were collected and filtered to train <span class=3D"ltx_text lt=
x_font_bold" id=3D"A1.F5.4.2.1">RLM-Qwen3-8B</span>. The left plots show th=
e unfiltered trajectories, and right plots show the post-filtering trajecto=
ries.</span></figcaption>
</figure><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report i=
ssue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A1.p5">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A1.p5.1">We used the <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_f=
ont_typewriter" id=3D"A1.p5.1.1">prime-rl</span> library&nbsp;<cite class=
=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Intellect, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"=
https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib56" title=3D"Prime rl library">2=
025</a>)</cite> for fine-tuning. We used a batch size of 64 for 300 trainin=
g steps, training for 48 H100 hours. While this exceedingly simple training=
 recipe was able to demonstrate substantial gains for our 8B model, we call=
 on future work to investigate training native RLMs much more thoroughly. W=
e expect that doing so at much larger scales in terms of model size, number=
 and variety of examples, and number of (ideally on-policy and online) roll=
outs will be necessary to maximize the potential of RLMs.</p><button class=
=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding ele=
ment</button>
</div>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_appendix" id=3D"A2">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Negative Results=
: Things we Tried that Did Not Work.</h2><button class=3D"sr-only button" s=
tyle=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A2.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A2.p1.1">Drawing inspiration from &nbsp;<cite clas=
s=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet">Redmon and Farhadi (<a class=3D"ltx_ref"=
 href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib45" title=3D"YOLOv3: an=
 incremental improvement">2018</a>)</cite>, we try to be descriptive about =
what tricks, quirks, and other relevant things failed and succeeded in a co=
ncise manner. Some observations are based on longer supplementary experimen=
ts, while others are based on small samples of results.</p><button class=3D=
"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding elemen=
t</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A2.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A2.p2.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=
=3D"A2.p2.1.1">Using the exact same RLM system prompt across all models can=
 be problematic.</span> We originally wrote the RLM system prompt with in c=
ontext examples for GPT-5, and tried to use the same system prompt for Qwen=
3-Coder, but found that it led to different, undesirable behavior in the tr=
ajectory. We had to add a small sentence to the RLM system prompt for Qwen3=
-Coder to prevent it from using too many recursive sub-calls.</p><button cl=
ass=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding =
element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A2.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A2.p3.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=
=3D"A2.p3.1.1">Models without sufficient coding capabilities struggle as RL=
Ms.</span> Our instantiation of RLMs relies on the ability to reason throug=
h and deal with the context in a REPL environment. We found from small scal=
e experiments that smaller models like Qwen3-8B&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cit=
e ltx_citemacro_citep">(Yang<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</=
span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib=
.bib44" title=3D"Qwen3 technical report">2025</a>)</cite> struggled without=
 sufficient coding abilities.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"=
display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A2.p4">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A2.p4.2"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=
=3D"A2.p4.2.1">Thinking models without sufficient output tokens struggle as=
 RLMs.</span> In addition to <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" i=
d=3D"A2.p4.2.2">Qwen3-Coder-480B-A35B-Instruct</span>, we also tried experi=
menting with <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" id=3D"A2.p4.2.3">=
Qwen3-235B-A22B</span> as the RLM. While we found positive results across t=
he board from the base model (e.g. on OOLONG&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite l=
tx_citemacro_citep">(Bertsch<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_bib_etal"> et al.</=
span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#bib=
.bib11" title=3D"Oolong: evaluating long context reasoning and aggregation =
capabilities">2025</a>)</cite>, performance jumped from <math alttext=3D"~3=
0\%" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"A2.p4.1.m1" intent=3D":lit=
eral"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"=
application/x-tex">~30\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math alttext=
=3D"~38\%" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"A2.p4.2.m2" intent=
=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>38</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation enco=
ding=3D"application/x-tex">~38\%</annotation></semantics></math>), the smal=
ler gap compared to the evaluated models in the main experiments (Table&nbs=
p;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#S3.T1" t=
itle=3D"Table 1 =E2=80=A3 3.1 Tasks =E2=80=A3 3 Scaling Long Context Tasks =
=E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1=
</span></a>) are due to multiple trajectories running out of output tokens =
while producing outputs due to thinking tokens exceeding the maximum output=
 token length of an individual LM call.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button"=
 style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A2.p5">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A2.p5.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=
=3D"A2.p5.1.1">RLMs without asynchronous LM calls are slow.</span> We imple=
mented all sub-LM queries naively as blocking / sequential calls, which cau=
sed our RLM experiments to be slow, especially compared to just the base mo=
del. We are confident that this can be resolved with a robust implementatio=
n.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issu=
e for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A2.p6">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A2.p6.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=
=3D"A2.p6.1.1">Depending on the model, distinguishing between a final answe=
r and a thought is brittle for RLMs.</span> The current strategy for distin=
guishing between a =E2=80=9Cnext turn" and a final answer for the RLM is to=
 have it wrap its answer in FINAL() or FINAL_VAR() tags. Similar to intuiti=
on about structured outputs degrading performance, we also found the model =
to make strange decisions (e.g. it outputs its plan as a final answer). We =
added minor safeguards, but we also believe this issue should be avoided al=
together in the future when models are trained as RLMs.</p><button class=3D=
"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding elemen=
t</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section class=3D"ltx_appendix" id=3D"A3">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Additional Metho=
ds and Baseline Details</h2><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"displ=
ay: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
<section class=3D"ltx_subsection" id=3D"A3.SS1">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Prompts for Experimen=
ts</h3><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report iss=
ue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A3.SS1.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A3.SS1.p1.1">We focus on methods that are entirely=
 task agnostic, so we fix our prompt for each method across all tasks. For =
the RLM prompt, the only difference between GPT-5 and Qwen3-Coder is an add=
ed line in the beginning that warns Qwen3-Coder not to use too many sub-LM =
calls =E2=80=93 we found in practice that without this warning, the model w=
ill try to perform a subcall on everything, leading to thousands of LM subc=
alls for basic tasks! For the fine-tuned Qwen3-8B experiment, we provide a =
slightly different prompt due to the differences in context window size of =
the smaller model (from 272k to 32k). In this section, we provide the syste=
m prompt used for all methods in =C2=A7<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https:/=
/arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#S3.SS1" title=3D"3.1 Tasks =E2=80=A3 3 Scaling=
 Long Context Tasks =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx=
_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> (other than the base model, which does no=
t include a system prompt).</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"di=
splay: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para ltx_noindent" id=3D"A3.SS1.p2">
