<section class=3D"ltx_subsection" id=3D"A4.SS2">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Scaling Huge Document=
 Corpuses in BrowseComp+</h3><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"disp=
lay: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A4.SS2.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A4.SS2.p1.3">In addition to the BrowseComp+&nbsp;<=
cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Chen<span class=3D"ltx_text lt=
x_bib_etal"> et al.</span>, <a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/=
html/2512.24601v2#bib.bib12" title=3D"BrowseComp-plus: a more fair and tran=
sparent evaluation benchmark of deep-research agent">2025</a>)</cite> resul=
ts for <math alttext=3D"k=3D1000" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=
=3D"A4.SS2.p1.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=3D<=
/mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">k=3D1000=
</annotation></semantics></math> documents in&nbsp;=C2=A7<a class=3D"ltx_re=
f" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2#S4" title=3D"4 Results and D=
iscussion =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_=
ref_tag">4</span></a>, we also include a smaller set of results on a subset=
 of <math alttext=3D"20" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"A4.SS2=
.p1.2.m2" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding=3D=
"application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> tasks from the origi=
nal <math alttext=3D"150" class=3D"ltx_Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"A4.SS=
2.p1.3.m3" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mn>150</mn><annotation encoding=
=3D"application/x-tex">150</annotation></semantics></math> to show how perf=
ormance degrades as a function of input size. In our original experiments, =
the base LMs were unable to handle the input contexts, so we add results to=
 show how they degrade. We include two new baselines, namely <span class=3D=
"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=3D"A4.SS2.p1.3.1">ReAct w/ GPT-5 + BM25</span> =
(a variant of the CodeAct baseline without access to a code environment) an=
d <span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold" id=3D"A4.SS2.p1.3.2">GPT-5 + pre-q=
uery BM25</span> (GPT-5 on pre-queried documents).</p><button class=3D"sr-o=
nly button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</bu=
tton>
</div>
<figure class=3D"ltx_figure" id=3D"A4.F6"><img alt=3D"Refer to caption" cla=
ss=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height=3D"201" id=3D"A4=
.F6.g1" src=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24601v2/figures/browsecomp-plus.=
png" width=3D"476">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure"><span class=3D"ltx_text" id=3D"A4.F6.2.1.1" style=3D"font-size:=
90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span class=3D"ltx_text" id=3D"A4.F6.3.2" sty=
le=3D"font-size:90%;">We plot the performance and API cost per answer of va=
rious methods using GPT-5 on 20 random queries in BrowseComp-Plus given inc=
reasing numbers of documents in context. Only the iterative methods (RLM, R=
eAct) maintain reasonable performance at 100+ documents.</span></figcaption=
>
</figure><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report i=
ssue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A4.SS2.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A4.SS2.p2.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold=
" id=3D"A4.SS2.p2.1.1">RLMs are able to scale well without performance degr=
adation.</span> RLM(GPT-5) is the only model / agent able to achieve and ma=
intain perfect performance at the 1000 document scale, with the ablation (n=
o recursion) able to similarly achieve <math alttext=3D"90\%" class=3D"ltx_=
Math" display=3D"inline" id=3D"A4.SS2.p2.1.m1" intent=3D":literal"><semanti=
cs><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-=
tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math> performance. The base GPT-5 model=
 approaches, regardless of how they are conditioned, show clear signs of pe=
rformance dropoff as the number of documents increase.</p><button class=3D"=
sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element=
</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A4.SS2.p3">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A4.SS2.p3.1"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold=
" id=3D"A4.SS2.p3.1.1">RLM inference cost scales reasonably.</span> The inf=
erence cost of RLMs on this setup scale log-linearly, and are reasonably bo=
unded compared to other common strategies like ReAct + BM25. If we extrapol=
ate the overall token costs of GPT-5 assuming it has an infinite context wi=
ndow, we observe that the inference cost of using RLM(GPT-5) is cheaper.</p=
><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for=
 preceding element</button>
</div>
