<section class=3D"ltx_appendix" id=3D"A5">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Additional RLM T=
rajectories</h2><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: none;">R=
eport issue for preceding element</button>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A5.p1">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A5.p1.1">In this section, we provide several examp=
le trajectories to highlight characteristics of frontier models as RLMs. Ma=
ny of the trajectories are too long to fit in text, so we describe each ste=
p and show specific examples when relevant.</p><button class=3D"sr-only but=
ton" style=3D"display: none;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
<div class=3D"ltx_para" id=3D"A5.p2">
<p class=3D"ltx_p" id=3D"A5.p2.1">A few noticeable properties of these traj=
ectories are that RLMs often make non-optimal choices despite their strong =
results in =C2=A7<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.2=
4601v2#S3" title=3D"3 Scaling Long Context Tasks =E2=80=A3 Recursive Langua=
ge Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. For example, =
in Example&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24=
601v2#A5.SS2" title=3D"E.2 RLM(Qwen3-Coder) on OOLONG-Pairs-Query_3 =E2=80=
=A3 Appendix E Additional RLM Trajectories =E2=80=A3 Recursive Language Mod=
els"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>, we observed that =
the RLM with Qwen3-Coder carefully constructs its final answer through a mi=
x of recursive sub-calls and code execution in the first iteration, but the=
n discards this information and continues wasting sub-calls before not usin=
g these stored answers. We also observed distinct differences in model beha=
vior such as in Example&nbsp;<a class=3D"ltx_ref" href=3D"https://arxiv.org=
/html/2512.24601v2#A5.SS3" title=3D"E.3 RLM(Qwen3-Coder) on OOLONG-Query_21=
2 =E2=80=A3 Appendix E Additional RLM Trajectories =E2=80=A3 Recursive Lang=
uage Models"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a>, where we =
found Qwen3-Coder make hundreds to thousands of recursive sub-calls for a s=
ingle simple task, while GPT-5 makes on the order of ten. While these examp=
les are not comprehensive, they provide useful qualitative insight into how=
 to improve RLMs.</p><button class=3D"sr-only button" style=3D"display: non=
e;">Report issue for preceding element</button>
</div>
